<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantinople</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantinople, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, was the city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> in the fourth century CE on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantion</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> for over a thousand years and one of the largest and richest cities of the medieval world, besieged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and others. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1453 and became the capital of the Ottoman Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
